WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.742
Checkpoint 2 asks for justification.

00:00:02.742 --> 00:00:05.677
This is more important than memorizing the label.

00:00:05.677 --> 00:00:09.962
Explain why ZIP code is nominal even when stored as an integer.

00:00:09.962 --> 00:00:15.712
Explain why Celsius supports meaningful differences but not meaningful ratios.

00:00:15.712 --> 00:00:21.462
Explain why arbitrary integer codes can mislead a model when categories are unordered.

00:00:21.462 --> 00:00:28.377
If you can explain the reason in ordinary language, you understand the concept well enough to apply it

00:00:28.377 --> 00:00:32.597
elsewhere. For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:32.597 --> 00:00:37.767
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:37.767 --> 00:00:41.427
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:41.427 --> 00:00:45.207
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:00:45.207 --> 00:00:52.067
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:00:52.067 --> 00:00:55.972
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project:

00:00:55.972 --> 00:01:00.482
validate the meaning of the result, not only whether the statement executes.
